# Defect 462's array, 2026-10-07. Symptom: an array read after its last
# release went on reading the freed block: four elements, released, then
# asked their length, answered from freed memory at exit 0 (4 in one run, 0
# in this case's, the allocator's bytes either way), and asked an element,
# read through a null descriptor and was told *a null pointer was read
# through*, which names a handle (measured on this Mac at `56def9b4`).
# Cause: the element descriptor, which the runtime sets to none before an
# array's block goes (defect 314), was asked by a new reference and a
# release and never by a read. Now every reader asks it
# (`hero_array_require`, `runtime/parts/array.c`), and the read is named.
#
# The array is the C's own, made through the runtime and read after its
# release by a function of the program's own header: a program cannot hand
# C an array's block, and the compiler never reads one it has released.
# AddressSanitizer names the read of the freed block.

extern "fixedbugs-462-an-array-read-after-its-last-release-stops-at-the-read.h"
    function read_after_release(elements: i64) -> i64

function main()
    print("four elements, given back")
    print(read_after_release(elements: 4))
